In API testing, 501 and 503 are both 5xx server-side errors, but they mean very different things.

Code	Meaning	When you get it	Typical testing example
501	Not Implemented	Server does not support the requested functionality/method	API doesn't support PATCH, OPTIONS, or some extension method
503	Service Unavailable	Server temporarily cannot handle requests	Server is down, under maintenance, overloaded, or a dependency/resource is unavailable
1. 501 — Not Implemented

Think:

"The server doesn't know/support how to do this."

For example, your API supports:

GET /users
POST /users
PUT /users
DELETE /users

But you send:

PATCH /users/123

and the server has no implementation for PATCH.

Request
   ↓
PATCH /users/123
   ↓
Server
   ↓
"PATCH functionality is not implemented"
   ↓
501 Not Implemented

HTTP specifies that 501 can be used when the server doesn't recognize/support the request method.
If the server does recognize the method but doesn't allow it for that particular resource,
405 Method Not Allowed is generally the appropriate response instead